atasette-ip-rate-limit/\",\n    \"published_date\": \"2026-05-14\",\n    \"topics\": [\"datasette\", \"rate-limiting\", \"codex\", \"infrastructure\", \"ai-assisted-programming\"],\n    \"type\": \"technical\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"title\": \"Welcome to the Datasette blog - built with OpenAI Codex desktop\",\n    \"description\": \"Launch of the official Datasette blog, built using OpenAI Codex desktop. Simon notes that Codex desktop has the Markdown session transcript export feature he's always wanted, and shares the session that built the blog. Demonstrates AI-assisted development of project infrastructure and communication channels.\",\n    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/May/13/datasette-blog/\",\n    \"published_date\": \"2026-05-13\",\n    \"topics\": [\"ai\", \"datasette\", \"generative-ai\", \"llms\", \"ai-assisted-programming\", \"codex\"],\n    \"type\": \"technical\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"title\": \"'11 AI agents' is meaningless as a phrase\",\n    \"description\": \"Boris Mann quote highlighted by Simon: 'If I said \\\"I have 11 spreadsheets\\\" or \\\"I have 11 browser tabs\\\" to do my work, it means about the same thing.' Critiques the vague terminology around AI agents and calls for more precise definitions and metrics when discussing agentic systems.\",\n    \"author\": \"Simon Willison (quoting Boris Mann)\",\n    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/May/13/ai-agents-meaningless/\",\n    \"published_date\": \"2026-05-13\",\n    \"topics\": [\"ai-agents\", \"ai\", \"agent-definitions\", \"terminology\"],\n    \"type\": \"opinion\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"title\": \"CSP Allow-list Experiment tool built with GPT-5.5\",\n    \"description\": \"Built a Content Security Policy experiment tool that demonstrates loading apps in CSP-protected sandboxed iframes with a custom fetch() that intercepts CSP errors and prompts users to add domains to an allow-list. Built with GPT-5.5 xhigh in the Codex desktop app. Shows practical exploration of security sandboxing and user-controlled CSP policies.\",\n    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/May/13/csp-allow-list/\",\n    \"published_date\": \"2026-05-13\",\n    \"topics\": [\"content-security-policy\", \"iframes\", \"security\", \"ai-assisted-programming\"],\n    \"type\": \"technical\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"title\": \"Datasette 1.0a29 with race condition fix\",\n    \"description\": \"Release of Datasette 1.0a29 featuring TokenRestrictions.abbreviated() utility, improved table headers for zero-row tables, Mobile Safari fixes, and a critical race condition bug fix. Simon used Codex CLI (GPT-5.5 xhigh) to create a minimal Dockerfile that recreated the gnarly segfault bug related to Database.close() timing. Demonstrates AI-assisted debugging and test environment creation.\",\n    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/May/12/datasette-1-0a29/\",\n    \"published_date\": \"2026-05-12\",\n    \"topics\": [\"projects\", \"datasette\", \"debugging\", \"testing\", \"ai-assisted-programming\"],\n    \"type\": \"technical\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"title\": \"LLM 0.32a2 with OpenAI responses endpoint support\",\n    \"description\": \"Release of LLM 0.32a2 with support for OpenAI's new /v1/responses endpoint (instead of /v1/chat/completions) for reasoning-capable models. This enables interleaved reasoning across tool calls for GPT-5 class models. Users can now see summarized reasoning tokens displayed in a different color, with -R/--hide-reasoning flags available. Important update for developers building with OpenAI's latest model capabilities.\",\n    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/May/12/llm-0-32a2/\",\n    \"published_date\": \"2026-05-12\",\n    \"topics\": [\"llm\", \"projects\", \"openai\", \"generative-ai\", \"ai\", \"llms\", \"reasoning-models\"],\n    \"type\": \"technical\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"title\": \"GitLab Act 2: Restructuring for the agentic era\",\n    \"description\": \"Analysis of GitLab's 'workforce reduction' and strategic changes for the agentic era: reducing countries with small teams by up to 30%, flattening organization by removing up to 3 management layers, reorganizing R&D into ~60 smaller empowered teams with end-to-end ownership. New values framework: 'Speed with Quality, Ownership Mindset, Customer Outcomes' (replacing CREDIT). Key insight: 'The agentic era multiplies demand for software' - as cost of producing software collapses, demand will expand. Simon notes this aligns with his Jevons-paradox-inspired optimism, though GitLab's stock drop suggests market uncertainty.\",\n    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/May/11/gitlab-act-2/\",\n    \"published_date\": \"2026-05-11\",\n    \"topics\": [\"37signals\", \"careers\", \"ai\", \"gitlab\", \"coding-agents\", \"jevons-paradox\", \"agentic-engineering\", \"organizational-design\"],\n    \"type\": \"opinion\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"title\": \"AI coding agents must reduce maintenance costs\",\n    \"description\": \"James Shore quote highlighted by Simon: 'Your AI coding agent needs to reduce your maintenance costs. You write code twice as quick now? Better hope you've halved your maintenance costs. Three times as productive? One third the maintenance costs. Otherwise, you're screwed.' Critical insight about the math of AI-assisted development: if you double output and double maintenance costs, you've quadrupled maintenance burden. The math only works if LLM decreases maintenance costs proportionally to increased output.\",\n    \"author\": \"Simon Willison (quoting James Shore)\",\n    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/May/11/ai-coding-maintenance-costs/\",\n    \"published_date\": \"2026-05-11\",\n    \"topics\": [\"coding-agents\", \"ai-assisted-programming\", \"generative-ai\", \"agentic-engineering\", \"ai\", \"llms\", \"software-engineering\"],\n    \"type\": \"opinion\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"title\": \"Your AI Use Is Breaking My Brain - The Zombie Internet\",\n    \"description\": \"Jason Koebler's excellent piece on how AI writing online is becoming impossible to avoid. Introduces 'Zombie Internet' concept: not just bots talking to bots, but 'people talking to bots, people talking to people, people creating AI agents and then instructing them to interact with people. It's people using AI talking to people who are not using AI, and it's people using AI talking to other people who are using AI.' Filtering AI content is mentally exhausting and it's starting to distort regular human writing styles. Simon highlights this as a key challenge in the evolving AI content landscape.\",\n    \"author\": \"Simon Willison (covering Jason Koebler)\",\n    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/May/11/zombie-internet/\",\n    \"published_date\": \"2026-05-11\",\n    \"topics\": [\"definitions\", \"ai\", \"generative-ai\", \"llms\", \"slop\", \"jason-koebler\", \"ai-ethics\", \"content-quality\"],\n    \"type\": \"opinion\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"title\": \"Using LLM in shebang lines for executable scripts\",\n    \"description\": \"TIL post about using LLM directly in shebang lines to create executable AI-powered scripts. Examples include: '#!/usr/bin/env -S llm -f' for prompts, '#!/usr/bin/env -S llm -T llm_time -f' for tool calls, and YAML templates for defining extra tools as Python functions. Demonstrates creative use of LLM tooling for creating executable AI scripts that can generate SVGs, write haikus with exact time, or perform calculations with tool calls.\",\n    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/May/11/llm-shebang/\",\n    \"published_date\": \"2026-05-11\",\n    \"topics\": [\"llm\", \"llm-tool-use\", \"llms\", \"ai\", \"generative-ai\", \"scripting\", \"developer-tools\"],\n    \"type\": \"technical\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"title\": \"Shopify's River - AI coding agent in public Slack channels\",\n    \"description\": \"Tobias Lütke describes Shopify's internal AI coding agent, River, which operates entirely in public on their Slack. River doesn't respond to DMs - it requires public channels, making all conversations searchable and allowing anyone to jump in. Tobias calls this 'Lehrwerkstatt' (teaching workshop) - 'The whole shop floor is the classroom. You learn by being near the work.' It's osmosis learning without requiring curriculum, training plans, or managers. Simon compares this to Midjourney's early success with public Discord channels forcing users to share prompts and learn from each other.\",\n    \"author\": \"Simon Willison (covering Tobias Lütke)\",\n    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/May/11/shopify-river-lehrwerkstatt/\",\n    \"published_date\": \"2026-05-11\",\n    \"topics\": [\"ai\", \"slack\", \"generative-ai\", \"llms\", \"midjourney\", \"coding-agents\", \"organizational-learning\", \"tobias-lutke\"],\n    \"type\": \"discussion\"\n  }\n]\n",
